<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE & METHOD CARDS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section content-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <h2 id="method-heading">How to Convert IST to EST</h2>
  <p class="method-intro">
    Because IST uses a half-hour offset (UTC+5:30), converting to EST (UTC-5) involves subtracting 10 full hours and then subtracting an additional 30 minutes.
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 1</p>
      <h3>Two-Step Subtraction Rule</h3>
      <p>
        First, subtract 10 hours from the IST time. Next, subtract 30 minutes. Example: 7:30 PM IST minus 10 hours = 9:30 AM, minus 30 minutes = 9:00 AM EST.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 2</p>
      <h3>Previous Day Calendar Rule</h3>
      <p>
        Any IST time prior to 10:30 AM falls into the previous calendar day when converted to EST. For example, 9:30 AM IST on Wednesday equals 11:00 PM EST on Tuesday evening.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 3</p>
      <h3>Summer Daylight Adjustment (EDT)</h3>
      <p>
        From mid-March to early November, the US East Coast uses Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4). Subtract 9 hours and 30 minutes from IST to get EDT.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

