<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting IST to EST</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Forgetting the 30-Minute Half-Hour Offset</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      The single most common error is treating the IST offset as a whole number of hours (such as 10 hours or 11 hours). Subtracting only 10 hours instead of 10.5 hours results in being 30 minutes late for meetings.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Overlooking North American Daylight Saving Time</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      India stays on UTC+5:30 all year. However, New York switches from EST (UTC-5) to EDT (UTC-4) in March. Failing to adjust the calculation to 9.5 hours during summer leads to one-hour scheduling errors.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Date Boundary Miscalculations for Morning IST</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Because EST is 10.5 hours behind, any IST timestamp prior to 10:30 AM occurs on the previous calendar day in Eastern time. For example, 8:00 AM IST on Thursday is 9:30 PM EST on Wednesday evening.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

